From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 13:34:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(web): preview, close and track labelled items in chat

Long agent sessions raise several items in one verbose reply, then refer
back to them across many short updates ("still need your call on the
cache question"). Finding the original detail means scrolling back or
spending a turn asking for a recap.

Item labels give those references a target. When a message tags an item
with a short bracketed label such as [Q2] or [R1], the first message
carrying that label defines it. Later mentions, in agent or user
messages, render as chips; clicking one opens a popover with the
defining block (its list item, heading section, table row, or
paragraph), plus a "Show in conversation" link that reuses the existing
?message= deep link.

A struck-through mention (~~[Q2]~~) closes the item: its chips are
struck everywhere and the popover adds the sentence that closed it. The
newest mention decides, so a later plain mention reopens it. A pinned
"Open items" panel above the thread lists open labels as chips and, when
expanded, every item with its title.

Labels only render once the transcript uses them (a label recurs across
messages, or one message tags several items), so a stray bracketed code
like [A4] in an unrelated session stays plain text.

Agents only label and close items when told to, so Omnigent adds a
framework instruction with the labelling and closing rules. SDK
harnesses get it every turn with the other framework instructions.
claude-native and codex-native take instructions only at launch, so the
static instruction is appended to the agent's own instructions on
--append-system-prompt and developer_instructions, for both
runner-created and CLI-launched sessions. Claude Code honours only the
last --append-system-prompt, so a user-supplied one is now merged into
the single flag rather than shadowed. codex-native relies on the
collaboration-mode fix so the always-present developer_instructions
no longer replace Plan mode's built-in instructions. Other native
harnesses have no instruction channel and are unchanged.

Not upstream yet: proposed from feat/chat-item-citations; drop this copy once it merges.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
